The sequence of the paper
The paper is for practitioners — tutors, educators, people who work with STEM students and know something is not working. The opening should name what they already experience: students who can pass exams but cannot explain what is happening one level below the answer. Students who have been through the content multiple times and still cannot reason through a novel problem. Tutors who explain correctly and the student nods and then fails the same question two weeks later. That is the gap this paper addresses and the reader needs to feel it before they care about your method.
Then the question is what comes second — and I would argue the contrast with conventional tutoring comes before any ingredient. Because the reader needs to understand what your method is not before they can hear what it is. If you open with kindness, a reader skims past it — every tutor thinks they are kind. If you open with the question bank, it sounds like a content tool. If you open with Socratic method, the reader thinks they already know what that means and they probably think they already do it.
The contrast resets their expectations. It tells them: the diagnosis of the problem is different here, and therefore everything that follows is different.
Then kindness. Because once the reader understands that the method works by exposing the student's shallow reasoning rather than transmitting correct content, the natural question is — how do you do that without damaging the student? And the answer to that question is kindness, which then unfolds into consent, curiosity, and charisma as you've worked it out.
The question bank and Socratic method come after the relational foundation is established, because those are tools, and tools in the hands of someone without the right disposition produce the wrong thing. A question bank used without genuine curiosity is an interrogation. Socratic method without kindness is humiliation. The reader needs to understand the disposition before the technique, or the technique will be misapplied.
So the rough sequence would be: the problem, the contrast with conventional tutoring, the relational foundation starting with kindness, then the tools and procedures. That order mirrors the logical dependency — you cannot use the tools correctly until you have the disposition, and you cannot understand why the disposition matters until you understand what the method is trying to do differently.
