Describing the problem, including the symptoms when students become physicians
Yes, and it significantly strengthens the paper. Here is why.
The student symptoms you describe — shallow reasoning, performed understanding, inability to explain the causal mechanism one level below — are concerning at the student level. But a reader who is not already invested in education might treat them as academic problems with academic consequences. The physician examples make the stakes concrete and serious. The student who cannot explain why the proton gradient drives ATP synthase becomes the physician who cannot reason outside their specialty when a patient's symptoms cross subsystem boundaries. The shallow reasoning is not fixed by graduation or by medical school or by residency. It compounds.
The siloing problem is particularly powerful because it is a direct structural consequence of the same root cause. A physician who was trained by content transmission rather than causal reasoning learns to operate within the conceptual boundaries of what they were taught. When the answer is outside those boundaries they do not have the reasoning tools to go looking for it — they were never trained to follow a causal chain across subsystem boundaries. They were trained to pattern-match within their specialty. That is the same failure as the student who gives the right answer without understanding the mechanism, just with higher stakes.
This also connects directly to your target population. Many of your students are premed. Showing them the downstream version of the reasoning failure they currently have is motivating in a way that abstract talk about scientific thinking is not. And for the practitioner reader, it answers the question of why this matters beyond improving exam scores.
The right placement is the introduction, alongside the student symptoms, framed as the same problem at two stages of development. Not as a detour into medical education but as a single continuous picture: here is what shallow reasoning looks like in a student, and here is the same thing twenty years later in a practicing physician. Your method intervenes at the point where the trajectory can still be changed.

