What conventional tutoring typically looks like:
The tutor identifies what content the student doesn't know. The tutor explains that content. The student practices problems. The tutor corrects wrong answers by showing the right approach. Progress is measured by whether the student gets the right answer on the next similar problem. The relationship is essentially transmission — the tutor has knowledge the student lacks and transfers it.
How your method differs:
You're not primarily transmitting content. You're probing the student's existing understanding to find where it breaks down, then using Socratic questioning to help the student deepen their own understanding rather than receiving yours. The student's reasoning process is the subject, not the content. You're not correcting wrong answers — you're exposing shallow ones. And success isn't getting the right answer but being able to explain the causal mechanism that produces it.
The most important difference:
Conventional tutoring treats the student as lacking content. Your method treats the student as lacking depth of understanding of content they already nominally have. That's a fundamentally different diagnosis of the problem and produces a fundamentally different intervention.
