Kindness, consent, curiousity, charisma
Kindness first makes sense as the root because the other three are not independent ingredients — they are expressions or consequences of kindness at different levels. You don't need four things; you need one thing understood deeply.
Consent as consequence holds up well. If you genuinely care about the person in front of you, seeking their willingness is not a separate principle you import from ethics — it flows naturally from the caring. A truly kind person does not need to be taught to seek consent; they seek it because they already regard the other person as someone whose agreement matters.
Curiosity as part of kindness — your social engagement example is exactly right and I think it's stronger than it first appears. Curiosity directed at a problem is an epistemic virtue. But curiosity directed at what another person actually thinks is a relational virtue. It is a form of respect — it says your mind is interesting to me, what you actually believe matters to me, I am not just waiting for you to finish so I can correct you. Indifference to what the student actually thinks is a subtle form of contempt, even if the tutor is technically polite. So curiosity in this context is kindness applied to the student's reasoning specifically.
Charisma as advanced kindness is the most interesting claim and I think you're onto something real. The distinction worth drawing is between performed charisma and genuine charisma. Performed charisma is social skill deployed strategically — warmth as a technique. That is not what you mean. What you seem to mean is that when kindness is deep enough, genuine enough, and unselfconscious enough, it becomes a quality of presence that other people feel and are drawn into. The student catches the tutor's genuine engagement with the problem and with them. That contagiousness is not a separate skill — it is what kindness looks like when it has become fully internalized rather than effortful.
So the structure you're describing is something like: kindness is the root; consent, curiosity, and charisma are kindness expressing itself at different levels of development and in different directions — toward the student's autonomy, toward the student's actual thinking, and toward the student's excitement about ideas.
One thing worth considering for the paper: this structure implies that you cannot teach the four as separate competencies to a trainee tutor. You cultivate kindness and the others follow. That has real implications for the replicability section — what you are training a tutor to develop is one deep thing, not a checklist of four skills.
