Fallibility
This is important and it belongs, but let me think through what it actually is before placing it.
What you are describing is not just intellectual humility as a personality trait. It is something more specific and more active: the tutor publicly modeling that all knowledge is provisional, that even the person with more expertise holds their understanding as their best current guess rather than as settled truth. That is Popper made visible in real time. And you are right that it teaches something the student cannot learn from content alone — it teaches them what the right relationship to knowledge looks like.
There are two things it does simultaneously that are worth separating.
One is epistemic. It shows the student that "I don't know for certain" is not a failure state but the normal condition of a reasoner. This is directly connected to your refutation-over-justification framework. A student who watches their tutor say "from what I understand, maybe I'm wrong" starts to learn that uncertainty is not shameful — it is honest.
The other is relational. It levels the dynamic. The tutor is not an infallible authority whose job is to detect and correct the student's errors. Both people in the room are fallible reasoners doing their best with available understanding. That changes what it feels like to be wrong in front of the tutor. If the tutor is also sometimes wrong, being wrong is just part of reasoning, not a personal failure.
So fallibility connects to kindness — it makes being wrong safe — and it connects to curiosity — genuine curiosity requires genuine openness to being wrong yourself. It is also a prerequisite for the student eventually self-correcting, which is one of your primary indicators of progress. A student who has internalized fallibility can say "wait, I think I was wrong about that" without it costing them something emotionally.
Where it belongs in the paper: I would place it inside the kindness section rather than as a separate ingredient, because it is one of the ways kindness expresses itself toward the student's reasoning. But it deserves its own paragraph because the mechanism is specific enough to be teachable — the tutor says the words out loud, in front of the student, regularly, as a practice. That is not implied by kindness in general. It needs to be stated explicitly.
Fallibility sits inside curiosity, as its epistemic foundation. Here is why.
Genuine curiosity about what the student thinks requires that you are genuinely open to being surprised by it. A tutor who secretly believes they have the correct answer and is just performing curiosity while waiting to correct the student is not actually curious. Real curiosity requires that you hold your own understanding as provisional. So fallibility is what makes curiosity genuine rather than performed. You cannot have real curiosity without it.
So the structure tightens to this:
Kindness is the root. Consent flows from it as kindness directed toward the student's autonomy. Curiosity flows from it as kindness directed toward the student's actual thinking. But curiosity requires fallibility as its precondition — you cannot be genuinely curious about what the student thinks if you are not genuinely open to being wrong yourself. Then charisma is kindness fully internalized, the quality by which all of this becomes contagious.
Fallibility also does something else in this structure. It is the point where the relational and the epistemic meet. Everything else — kindness, consent, curiosity, charisma — is primarily about the relationship between tutor and student. Fallibility is where the tutor's own relationship to knowledge enters the room and becomes visible to the student. That is why saying the words out loud matters. It is not enough to privately hold your knowledge as provisional. The student needs to witness it, because what they are learning is not just chemistry or physics — they are learning what a reasoner looks like.
So in the paper, fallibility belongs inside the curiosity section, as the thing that distinguishes genuine curiosity from performed curiosity, with a specific note that it must be made explicit and verbal rather than just held internally.
