Feynman's central move in that speech is to stand in front of the people who are supposed to represent scientific achievement and say: we have failed to apply real scientific thinking to the questions closest to us, including how we teach and how we learn. That is an act of public fallibility from one of the most credentialed scientists alive at the time. The form of the speech is itself an example of what you are advocating. That is worth noting because it makes the endnote do double work — it references intellectual content and models the disposition simultaneously.
The specific content connects in three places in your paper.
First, his first principle — you must not fool yourself, and you are the easiest person to fool — is the epistemological foundation of your fallibility point. The tutor who says "from what I understand, maybe I'm wrong" is practicing Feynman's first principle out loud in front of the student. That is an endnote connection worth making explicit.
Second, his concept of cargo cult science connects directly to your distinction between genuine and performed understanding. A student who can produce correct answers without understanding the causal mechanism is doing cargo cult reasoning — they have the outward form without the substance. Feynman named the phenomenon at the level of scientific institutions; you are describing the same phenomenon at the level of individual student cognition.
Third, his apology — that educational research has consistently failed to produce reliable knowledge about what actually works — is a direct endorsement of the gap your paper is filling, even though he never proposed a solution. That belongs in your introduction as an endnote, not to claim Feynman's authority for your method, but to show that the problem you are addressing was visible to serious thinkers long before you wrote about it.
The one thing to be careful about is not overclaiming the connection. Feynman was diagnosing a failure; he was not proposing a Socratic method or anything like your framework. The endnote should present him as a clear-eyed identifier of the problem rather than a precursor to your solution. The solution is yours and Popper's, not Feynman's.
