What is missing
1. Session structure — the actual sequence of a session
Your notes describe what the method is philosophically but not what it looks like in practice minute by minute. A practitioner reader needs to know: How does a session open? Do you ask the student to explain a concept cold, or do you give them a problem first? How long do you probe before hinting? What signals tell you to shift from probing to hinting to direct correction? The paper promises a cooking recipe and this is the missing procedure section.
2. The three modes and when to switch between them
Your key methods list mentions probing, hinting, and direct correction as distinct moves. Your notes don't describe any of them in detail, don't explain what triggers a switch from one to another, and don't explain whether direct correction is ever appropriate or always a last resort. This is one of the most replicable and teachable parts of the method and it's entirely absent.
3. What shallow reasoning concretely looks like
"Shallow reasoning" is the central diagnostic concept of the paper, but your notes don't give examples of it. A tutor reading this paper needs to be able to recognize shallow reasoning when they hear it. What does a student say that tells you the answer is performed rather than understood? Contrast examples — shallow answer versus deep answer for the same question — would make this immediately teachable.
4. The distinction between description and explanation
This is listed as a key method but is completely absent from your notes. This distinction is probably the most important conceptual tool a tutor needs. "The mitochondria produces ATP" is description. Explaining why the proton gradient drives ATP synthase is explanation. The paper needs to define this distinction precisely and show a tutor how to probe for it.
5. The causal mechanism criterion
You mention it in the contrast section — success means explaining the causal mechanism one level below the answer — but you don't develop it as an operational standard. What counts as "one level below"? How does the tutor know when they've reached adequate depth versus when they should push further? This needs to be spelled out with examples across the four subject areas.
6. The question bank as an ingredient
It's listed as ingredient #1 in your outline but appears nowhere in your notes. Even a brief description of what kinds of questions belong in the bank, how they are structured, and what reasoning failure each category is designed to expose would fill a significant gap. Sample questions from physics, chemistry, and biology are probably necessary for a practitioner journal.
7. How to tell genuine understanding from performed understanding
This is listed as a key method and it's arguably the hardest skill in the whole approach. Your notes don't describe any of the signals. What does a student do or say that tells you understanding is genuine? What are the tells of performance? Presumably genuine understanding includes the ability to generate novel analogies, catch their own errors, apply the concept to a new context. This needs to be described explicitly.
8. The role of analogy hints
Analogy hints are mentioned in your key methods but absent from your notes. When do you use an analogy instead of a question? How do you construct an analogy that hints without transmitting? This is a distinct tutoring move and it needs its own description.
9. Explicit versus intuitive knowledge
Your project description says students are treated as already possessing relevant knowledge in both explicit and intuitive forms. This is a non-obvious and important claim — the method depends on it. But your notes don't describe how to access intuitive knowledge, how to help a student articulate something they know implicitly, or why this is different from a student who genuinely doesn't know something at all. Without this, the claim that the student already has the raw material reads as an assertion rather than a procedure.
10. The filtering and alignment process
Ingredient #1 in your notes is the pre-session alignment between parent, student, and tutor. This is mentioned but not described. What does misalignment look like? What does the tutor need to communicate before the first session? What expectations need to be set? For a practitioner paper this is where implementation often fails, and a reader needs guidance.
11. Indicators of progress with enough detail to use them
Your project description lists four indicators: student self-correction, student excitement and voluntary adoption, student discussing ideas outside the session, and transfer to new domains. Your notes don't mention any of these. These are what make the paper's success criteria concrete, and without them the paper can't tell a tutor whether the method is working.
12. What to do when a student is completely stuck
The paper describes what to do when a student gives a shallow answer. It doesn't describe what to do when a student gives no answer at all, shuts down, or becomes visibly frustrated. A practitioner needs a protocol for this. Even a brief treatment of how to retrieve a stuck student without reverting to direct transmission would address a common failure mode.
13. Replicability and training requirements
Your outline includes this in the discussion section. Your notes don't address it at all. What background knowledge does a tutor need? Can someone with subject matter knowledge but no training in Socratic method use this? What is the hardest disposition to develop? This is important for a practitioner journal because editors will ask whether the method can be taught to others or whether it depends on your particular personality and experience.
